---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Stateful Applications with AttoLang — WebSockets, Actors, Messaging"
  description="Build WebSocket servers, gaming backends, interactive UIs, distributed state machines, message queues, and development servers with AttoLang's fault-tolerant actor runtime."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Applications / Stateful systems</p>
    <h1>Build the applications that stay alive.</h1>
    <p>
      AttoLang was motivated by the need to create more stateful applications, as opposed to
      stateless REST APIs: WebSocket servers, gaming backends, UIs, state machines for distributed
      coordination, message queues, and development servers.
    </p>
    <p>
      AttoLang takes the best from Erlang and Rust and projects like Microsoft Orleans and offers
      a next-generation language and runtime for applications built around persistent state,
      continuous messaging, and fault-tolerant actors.
    </p>
    <p>
      We can deliver more than <strong>10× the speed of Erlang</strong> while using
      <strong>one-tenth the compute</strong>, with the type safety and memory safety that languages
      like Rust offer.
    </p>
    <p>
      AttoLang offers full AOT compilation, native binaries for all platforms, zero GC,
      fault-tolerant actors, and better hot upgrades.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="page-body technical-page stateful-applications-page">
    <div class="prose">
      <nav class="bench-jump" aria-label="Stateful application showcase">
        <a href="#application-types">Applications</a>
        <a href="#persistent-actors">Persistent actors</a>
        <a href="#messaging">Messaging</a>
        <a href="#fault-tolerance">Fault tolerance</a>
        <a href="#hot-upgrades">Hot upgrades</a>
        <a href="#native-deployment">Native deployment</a>
      </nav>

      <section class="benchmark-section" id="application-types">
        <p class="eyebrow">One runtime / many stateful workloads</p>
        <h2>Applications that need state, not just endpoints.</h2>
        <p>
          Keep sessions, domain state, behavior, and messaging together. AttoLang is built for
          systems that stay active between requests and react continuously to users, devices,
          other services, and events.
        </p>
        <div class="schedule-grid">
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>WebSocket servers</strong>
            <p>Manage long-lived connections, user sessions, presence, subscriptions, rooms, and real-time event delivery.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Gaming backends</strong>
            <p>Keep matches, lobbies, player sessions, world state, leaderboards, and game events close to the actors that own them.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Interactive UIs</strong>
            <p>Power live dashboards, collaborative interfaces, server-driven UI state, notifications, and reactive subscriptions.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Distributed state machines</strong>
            <p>Coordinate workflows, leases, long-running processes, state transitions, and multi-actor business operations.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Message queues and streams</strong>
            <p>Build bounded mailboxes, pub-sub topics, fan-out pipelines, backpressure, and reliable message-driven services.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Development servers</strong>
            <p>Run file watchers, incremental builds, live diagnostics, local service graphs, and hot-reloaded development tools.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="benchmark-section" id="persistent-actors">
        <p class="eyebrow">State + behavior / one ownership boundary</p>
        <h2>Persistent actors keep application state close to the work.</h2>
        <p>
          A connection, room, user, game session, queue worker, or workflow can have an actor
          responsible for its state and behavior. Other parts of the application communicate
          through typed messages instead of sharing arbitrary mutable state.
        </p>
        <ul class="runtime-flow" aria-label="Persistent actor request lifecycle">
          <li>
            <strong>Connect</strong>
            <span>A WebSocket gateway accepts the connection and resolves its session or room.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Route</strong>
            <span>The gateway sends typed messages to the actor that owns the relevant state.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Process</strong>
            <span>The actor applies commands, updates its own state, and publishes domain events.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Respond</strong>
            <span>Replies and subscriptions deliver results to clients and other actors.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Continue</strong>
            <span>The actor remains available for the next message, timer, connection event, or workflow transition.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="benchmark-section" id="messaging">
        <p class="eyebrow">Communication / backpressure / flow control</p>
        <h2>Messaging is the application fabric.</h2>
        <p>
          Actors communicate through messages, channels, and pub-sub topics. Messages carry
          data across ownership boundaries; the runtime coordinates delivery, queue limits,
          cancellation, and backpressure so a slow consumer does not silently turn into an
          unbounded memory problem.
        </p>
        <div class="schedule-grid">
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Typed actor messages</strong>
            <p>Make the data crossing an actor boundary explicit and keep mutable state with its owner.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Channels and selection</strong>
            <p>Coordinate asynchronous work across producers, consumers, timers, and cancellation paths.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Pub-sub and actor groups</strong>
            <p>Publish domain events to groups of subscribers and manage subscription lifecycles.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Bounded flow</strong>
            <p>Use quotas, backpressure, and explicit delivery policies to keep queues under control.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="benchmark-section" id="fault-tolerance">
        <p class="eyebrow">Supervision / recovery / isolation</p>
        <h2>Fault tolerance belongs in the runtime.</h2>
        <p>
          A supervisor owns the actors it manages and applies restart and recovery policies
          when an actor fails. Actor boundaries keep state and failure domains explicit;
          one session or worker does not need to become one process or one microservice.
        </p>
        <div class="schedule-grid">
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Supervised actor groups</strong>
            <p>Organize related actors under a supervisor and make recovery policy part of the application architecture.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Isolated state</strong>
            <p>Keep mutable state actor-owned and move data through messages with explicit ownership rules.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Controlled scheduling</strong>
            <p>Coordinate asynchronous handlers, critical state access, timeouts, cancellation, and bounded mailboxes.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="schedule-tile">
            <strong>Recovery without service sprawl</strong>
            <p>Use actor supervision and isolation inside the runtime instead of splitting every stateful component into a separate service by default.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="benchmark-section" id="hot-upgrades">
        <p class="eyebrow">Hot code / generations / rollback</p>
        <h2>Upgrade a running system without losing its identity.</h2>
        <p>
          AttoLang's hot-upgrade model uses actor generations so a service can introduce new
          code while keeping actor references stable. Stage the new generation, move work
          deliberately, and retain a rollback path.
        </p>
        <ul class="runtime-flow" aria-label="Hot upgrade lifecycle">
          <li>
            <strong>Stage</strong>
            <span>Load the next code generation and prepare its runtime resources.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Validate</strong>
            <span>Check compatibility, ownership boundaries, and activation requirements.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Quiesce</strong>
            <span>Stop admitting new work to the old generation and coordinate in-flight work.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Activate</strong>
            <span>Route new work to the new generation while preserving stable actor identity.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Commit or roll back</strong>
            <span>Retire the old generation after success, or restore it when activation fails.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="benchmark-section" id="native-deployment">
        <p class="eyebrow">AOT / native binaries / zero GC</p>
        <h2>One language for development and native deployment.</h2>
        <p>
          AttoLang brings full ahead-of-time compilation, native binaries for all platforms,
          zero GC, Rust-like type and memory safety, fault-tolerant actors, and hot upgrades
          into one language and runtime. Stateful services can keep their actor architecture
          from local development through native deployment.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/performance/">Explore AttoLang performance and benchmark coverage →</a>
          · <a href="/erlang-rust-oreslang/">Compare Erlang, Rust, and AttoLang →</a>
          · <a href="/how-it-works/">Read how the runtime works →</a>
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
